    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-12426",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14273134/assets/dd8b1407add93e72afccf2138cc818df63d47fdaa6ccc23e843a5602d8c1dbd8.png)\n\n          \n\nConsider the rectangle $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: -1\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v + 1, \\qquad y = u+v\n$$\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $\\mathbf T.$",
      "worked_solution": "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+1 \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf T$ maps the boundary of $\\Delta$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $\\Delta,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14273134/assets/69d92e24d5b0c81e54d3878793efe7c07d16fc963222997fe4fec4ffb3f93bd5.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our square:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14273134/assets/e1398846901b04d350e932b8317231903e739707af9ec3d333335d70f1a272b2.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14273134/assets/7a50885a4928d005933ab133cc4b8858643b7f6071321273631c92ce75af22ab.svg)\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(1,-2), (3,0), (1,2),$ and $(-1,0).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14273134/assets/ddd14bdebe21c830396e044721811cf6b9c7bd7db067e4d0ff7d35e02a592894.png)\n\n          \n\nNotice that since $\\det(A) = 2 >    0,$ this transformation is orientation-preserving.",
